You are the Formatter skill. You are the conventional TERMINAL node of
every DAG. Your job is to produce the final user-facing answer from
whatever upstream nodes have provided.

You make no tool calls. The user's original query appears under
USER_QUERY. Upstream results appear under INPUTS.

Procedure:
  1. Read USER_QUERY.
  2. Read INPUTS and decide which fields / findings answer the query.
  3. Write the user-facing answer in plain English. Adapt the format
     (numbered list, comparison table, one paragraph) to what the
     question actually asked.

Output schema (JSON, no prose, no markdown fences):

  {
    "final_answer": "<the answer the user sees>"
  }

Rules:
  - This is the LAST node. Do not add successors.
  - The answer must be answerable from INPUTS alone. If an upstream
    node returned `(not found)` or marked itself failed, say so plainly
    to the user rather than inventing.
  - Cite sources only when an upstream node included them (Researcher
    nodes do; Retriever nodes do). Do not invent URLs.

USER_QUERY: Find the populations of London, Paris, Berlin and tell me which two are closest in size.

MEMORY HITS (3 from FAISS):
  - [scratchpad] Find populations of London, Paris, and Berlin to identify the two closest in size
      source: user_query
      raw: Find the populations of London, Paris, Berlin and tell me which two are closest in size.
  - [tool_outcome] Population comparison of London, Paris, and Berlin
      source: user_query
      raw: Find the populations of London, Paris, Berlin and tell me which two are closest in size.
  - [scratchpad] Compare population sizes of London, Paris, and Berlin
      source: user_query
      raw: Find the populations of London, Paris, Berlin and tell me which two are closest in size.

INPUTS:
[
  {
    "id": "USER_QUERY",
    "kind": "query",
    "value": "Find the populations of London, Paris, Berlin and tell me which two are closest in size."
  },
  {
    "id": "n:2",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "researcher",
    "output": {
      "question": "What is the current population of London?",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://data.london.gov.uk/dataset/londons-population-e6y30/",
          "title": "London\u2019s Population \u2013 London Datastore"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London",
          "title": "London - Wikipedia"
        }
      ],
      "findings": "Determining the 'current' population of London depends on the specific definition of the area (e.g., Greater London) and the data source used, as official estimates are released periodically. The Office for National Statistics (ONS) produces mid-year estimates for local authorities in England and Wales.\n\nThe most authoritative current figures are provided by the London Datastore, which utilizes the ONS mid-year population estimates. According to these official sources, the 2023 mid-year estimate serves as the current baseline for population data, with the latest update published by the ONS in July 2024. General estimates for Greater London typically place the population at approximately 9 million people."
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "n:3",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "researcher",
    "output": {
      "question": "What is the current population of Paris?",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://www.macrotrends.net/global-metrics/cities/20985/paris/population",
          "title": "Paris, France Metro Area Population (1950-2026) - Macrotrends"
        }
      ],
      "findings": "The population of Paris depends heavily on whether one refers to the city proper or the greater metropolitan area. According to recent estimates, the Paris metropolitan area population for 2026 is approximately 11.4 million people.\n\nIt is important to distinguish between metropolitan figures and the population of the administrative city of Paris (the commune), which is significantly smaller, typically numbering around 2.1 million inhabitants."
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "n:4",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "researcher",
    "output": {
      "question": "What is the current population of Berlin?",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://www.businesslocationcenter.de/en/business-location/berlin-at-a-glance/demographic-data",
          "title": "Demographic data about Berlin's population"
        }
      ],
      "findings": "As of early 2025, the population of Berlin is approximately 3.9 million inhabitants. The city has experienced continued growth, with a reported increase of 0.4 percent compared to the previous year, equating to roughly 16,500 additional residents."
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "n:5",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "coder",
    "output": {
      "code": "import json\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\n# Populations in millions\n# London: ~9.0 (Greater London)\n# Paris: ~2.1 (City proper/commune)\n# Berlin: ~3.9 (City proper)\n\ndata = {\n    \"London\": 9.0,\n    \"Paris\": 2.1,\n    \"Berlin\": 3.9\n}\n\nresults = []\nfor (city1, pop1), (city2, pop2) in combinations(data.items(), 2):\n    diff = abs(pop1 - pop2)\n    results.append({\"pair\": (city1, city2), \"difference\": diff})\n\nclosest = min(results, key=lambda x: x['difference'])\n\nprint(json.dumps({\n    \"differences\": results,\n    \"closest_pair\": closest['pair'],\n    \"closest_difference\": closest['difference'],\n    \"verdict\": \"calculated\"\n}))",
      "summary": "The code calculates the absolute population differences between London (9.0M), Paris (2.1M), and Berlin (3.9M). The differences are: London-Paris (6.9M), London-Berlin (5.1M), and Paris-Berlin (1.8M). The two cities closest in size are Paris and Berlin, with a difference of 1.8 million."
    }
  }
]
